{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":24,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal_link_additive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal_link_additive","kind":"theorem","line":271,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L271-L281","doc":"The diagonal envelope inherits exact link-additivity from `b₁`: adding one\ninter-nucleon link (`k` crossings) adds exactly `½ · k · κ²`, independent of\n`A`.  Formal witness that the diagonal reading is additive (pairwise), not\nsuper-pairwise. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal_flat","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal_flat","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L265-L269","doc":"**Flat curvature (`F_q = 0 ⇒ κ² = 0`) ⇒ zero magnitude** — bosonic /\nq-flat / cocommutative case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal_trefoil","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal_trefoil","kind":"theorem","line":258,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L258-L263","doc":"Deuteron diagonal envelope is exactly `κ²` (`b₁ = 2`, `½·2·κ² = κ²`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverseMagnitudeDiagonal","kind":"def","line":254,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L254-L256","doc":"Diagonal transverse-magnitude envelope `½ · b₁ · κ²`, `κ² = ‖F_q‖²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_trace_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_trace_zero","kind":"theorem","line":231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L231-L242","doc":"**The whole transverse pairing has vanishing trace/character.**  Under any\ncyclic functional (Markov trace, block character `χ^λ`) the ordered\ntransverse pairing evaluates to `0`.  So the transverse surface curvature is\na purely *operator-valued* object; it leaves no imprint on the scalar Jones/\nMarkov trace. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.rowPairing_trace_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.rowPairing_trace_zero","kind":"theorem","line":223,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L223-L229","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm_trace_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm_trace_zero","kind":"theorem","line":217,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L217-L221","doc":"A cyclic linear functional kills the curvature commutator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverse_nonzero_needs_noncommuting_curvature","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.transverse_nonzero_needs_noncommuting_curvature","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L189-L203","doc":"**THE WALL (formalised).**  A *nonzero* transverse surface curvature\n**forces** two cells whose transported curvatures do not commute.  Since\nthe germ curvature `F_q` is a single universal substrate element, distinct\nnon-commuting `𝓕ᵢ = U(xᵢ)⁻¹ F_q U(xᵢ)` can only arise from *position-\ndependent transport* `U(xᵢ)` across the surface — exactly the canonical\nYekutieli surface-2-form discretisation that the corpus lacks (bean\n`qou-prjo`).  So the transverse magnitude is unpinned without that data:\nthis theorem is the precise statement of why the route is walled, not a\nderivation of the magnitude. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_const","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_const","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L179-L187","doc":"**Uniform (flat-transport) curvature ⇒ vanishes.**  The special case where\nevery cell carries the *same* curvature `c` (trivial transport, no handle\ntwist): the transverse pairing is `0`.  A nonzero transverse surface\ncurvature therefore cannot arise from a flat surface. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_eq_zero_of_all_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_eq_zero_of_all_comm","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L163-L177","doc":"**Flat / commuting surface ⇒ transverse curvature vanishes.**  If all pairs\nof local curvatures commute (`⟦𝓕ᵢ, 𝓕ⱼ⟧ = 0`) — in particular the\n*flat-transport* case where every `𝓕ᵢ` equals a common `F_q` — the whole\ntransverse pairing is zero.  This is the discrete form of the germ-curvature\ndichotomy \"agreement ⟺ flatness\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.rowPairing_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.rowPairing_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L154-L161","doc":"If every later curvature commutes with the fixed earlier one, that row\nvanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_pair","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L148-L152","doc":"Two cells: the pairing is the single ordered commutator `⟦𝓕₁, 𝓕₀⟧`\n(later with earlier) — manifestly nonzero for non-commuting curvatures,\nso the transverse object is **not vacuously zero**. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_singleton","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_singleton","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L145-L146","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_nil","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing_nil","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L143-L143","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.listPairing","kind":"def","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L135-L141","doc":"**Transverse surface-curvature pairing** (discrete `½ P ∮∮ [𝓕,𝓕]`).\nOrdered double sum over the surface cells: each earlier cell `c` paired\nwith all later cells, then recurse.  Argument is the list of transported\nlocal curvatures `[𝓕₀, 𝓕₁, …]` in surface order. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.rowPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.rowPairing","kind":"def","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L129-L133","doc":"Row contribution: the ordered commutators of every *later* cell curvature\n`y ∈ ys` with a fixed *earlier* one `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm_antisymm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm_antisymm","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L122-L127","doc":"The germ curvature is a genuine antisymmetric 2-form: swapping the two\nobservers negates it (`germ-commutator-q-curvature.md` consequence 2).\n`R` is a generic (possibly non-commutative) ring, so `noncomm_ring`\nrather than `ring`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm_self","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L119-L120","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.curvComm","kind":"def","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L116-L117","doc":"Curvature commutator `⟦a,b⟧ = a·b − b·a` — the discrete `[𝓕,𝓕]` integrand. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_extensive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_extensive","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L94-L103","doc":"**Extensivity.**  With the framework strand count `s = 3A` (each nucleon a\n3-strand Borromean baryon) and crossing density `ρ` per nucleon\n(`c = ρ·A`), the Betti number is `b₁ = (ρ − 3)·A + 1` — **extensive**\n(grows with `A`) precisely when `ρ > 3`, i.e. when the crossing density\nexceeds the strand density.  This is the forward geometric statement that\nthe transverse surface curvature *can* carry an `∝ A` (extensive) binding\nterm — the shape the corpus's missing additive term must have. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_link_additive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_link_additive","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L81-L92","doc":"**Link-additivity (the pairwise cap).**  Adding `k` crossings to a fixed\nstrand set (one inter-nucleon link, *no* new strands) increments `b₁` by\n**exactly** `k`.  Hence the Betti/diagonal transverse magnitude is\n*exactly linear* in the number of inter-nucleon links — additive,\nnever super-additive.  This is the structural reason the Betti-count\nreading **cannot** be the ≥3-body super-pairwise term the corpus proves\nis required (`deltalambda…additive-term.md` §4): a genuinely 3-body\nenhancement must come from the *off-diagonal* cross-handle pairing\n(Part B), not from `b₁`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_disk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_disk","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L75-L79","doc":"**Unknot / disk (planar, genus 0) has `b₁ = 0`.**  For `c = s − 1` (a tree\nof bands) the surface is a disk — no transverse handle.  E.g. `s = 2`,\n`c = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_trefoil","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne_trefoil","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L70-L73","doc":"**Deuteron / trefoil.**  The pn channel word `σ₁³` is `s = 2`, `c = 3`, so\n`b₁ = 2` (genus one).  A concrete, falsifiable value — not a definition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TransverseSurfaceCurvature.seifertBettiOne","kind":"def","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TransverseSurfaceCurvature.lean#L66-L68","doc":"First Betti number `b₁ = c − s + 1 = 2g` of the Seifert surface of a\nconnected `s`-strand, `c`-crossing braid diagram. ","signatureChunk":0}]}